#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065337 :tutorial/content "The **quotient rule for exponents** states that when we divide two exponents that have the same base, we take the difference of the exponents in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m}=a^{n-m}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a^{5}}{a^{2}} &=  \\\\\na^{(5 - 2)} &=  \\\\\n&= a^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see why the quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.\n\nFor the two terms $a^5$ and $a^2$ we have\n\n$$\na^5 = a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\na^2 = a \\cdot a\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute these two expressions into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a^5}{a^2}\n$$\n\nwe get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{a^{5}}{a^2}&=\\ \\dfrac {a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {a \\cdot a}&=\\ \\dfrac {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a}} \\\\ &= a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\\\ &= a^3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ce1b58b-8395-5986-99e6-cfd10bbca653" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1478 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
